<div class="container-narrow">
  <h1 class="viewer-title">A world that programs itself</h1>

  <div class="viewer"
       data-controller="viewer"
       data-viewer-worlds-value="<%= @show.worlds.to_json %>"
       data-viewer-substrate-value="<%= @show.substrate %>"
       data-viewer-seed-value="<%= @show.seed %>"
       data-viewer-steps-per-frame-value="<%= @show.steps_per_frame %>"
       data-viewer-wasm-url-value="<%= @show.wasm_url %>">
    <canvas class="viewer-canvas" data-viewer-target="canvas" aria-label="The living world"></canvas>

    <p class="viewer-status text-sm text-error" data-viewer-target="status" role="status"></p>

    <div class="viewer-controls">
      <div class="form-group viewer-control">
        <label class="form-label text-xs" for="viewer-substrate">Substrate</label>
        <select class="form-select" id="viewer-substrate" data-action="viewer#changeSubstrate">
          <% @show.substrates.each do |substrate| %>
            <option value="<%= substrate %>" <%= "selected" if substrate == @show.substrate %>>
              <%= substrate.capitalize %>
            </option>
          <% end %>
        </select>
      </div>

      <div class="form-group viewer-control">
        <label class="form-label text-xs" for="viewer-speed">Speed</label>
        <input class="viewer-speed" id="viewer-speed" type="range" min="1" max="8" step="1"
               value="<%= @show.steps_per_frame %>" data-action="viewer#changeSpeed">
      </div>

      <div class="viewer-buttons">
        <button class="btn btn-primary" type="button" data-action="viewer#toggle">
          <span data-viewer-target="playLabel">Pause</span>
        </button>
        <button class="btn btn-outline" type="button" data-action="viewer#step">Step</button>
        <button class="btn btn-outline" type="button" data-action="viewer#reset">New seed</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <dl class="viewer-readout">
      <div class="viewer-metric">
        <dt class="text-xs text-muted uppercase">Epoch</dt>
        <dd class="code" data-viewer-target="epoch">0</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="viewer-metric">
        <dt class="text-xs text-muted uppercase">Compression</dt>
        <dd class="code" data-viewer-target="compressRatio">—</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="viewer-metric">
        <dt class="text-xs text-muted uppercase">Replicators</dt>
        <dd class="code" data-viewer-target="replicatorCount">—</dd>
      </div>
      <div class="viewer-metric">
        <dt class="text-xs text-muted uppercase">Distinct tapes</dt>
        <dd class="code" data-viewer-target="distinctTapes">—</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </div>

  <div class="viewer-copy">
    <p>
      Every cell above holds a tiny program — 64 random bytes. Each epoch, a cell meets one of
      its neighbours, the two tapes are glued together and the result is executed as code. The
      colour of a cell is a fingerprint of the bytes it holds, its brightness the share of those
      bytes that are instructions. Nothing here selects for anything: there is no fitness
      function, no reward, no reproduction rule. The programs are the only thing that can change
      the programs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Watch the colours. A random soup is confetti, every cell a different tape, and the
      compression ratio sits near 1.0. Sooner or later one program copies itself into its
      neighbour, that neighbour copies it on, and the confetti collapses into a few broad
      stretches of one colour — life, or at least the part of it that replicates. The
      compression ratio falls, the replicator count climbs and the number of distinct tapes
      drops. The same engine runs the sweeps that measure when this happens, in
      <a href="/experiments">the experiments</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
